2 Related work

Syntactic Language Models. There have been

extensive studies on syntactic language model-

ing (Baker, 1979;

Jelinek and Lafferty, 1991;

Chelba, 1997;

Chelba and Jelinek, 2000;

Vinyals

et al., 2015;

Charniak et al., 2016;

Dyer et al., 2016;

Qian et al., 2021), in which words and constituent

symbols are mixed up and generated in a left-to-

right manner. Recent works (Sartran et al., 2022;

Murty et al., 2023) utilize Transformers to param-

eterize action probability distributions, but relies

on annotated parse trees or parsers trained on gold

trees as structural guidance. Besides, unsupervised

SLMs are also explored, by differentiable struc-

tured hidden layers (Kim et al., 2017;

Shen et al.,

2018, 2019a;

DuSell and Chiang, 2024), reinforce-

ment learning approaches (Yogatama et al., 2017),

or variational approximations (Li et al., 2019;

